      "form_note": "h33-h33-2-b2; SNAP cross-strike cap 2px, eps x0.25 (TMI+grav hg consensus); pred +0.00 DTI MODEL",
      "mechanism_card": {
        "hypothesis": "Correcting cross-strike position by up to 2 px moves dots toward the strongest magnetic/gravity gradient edge, which is where a mapped fault trace is most likely to lie; because kernel credit is linear in distance, this raises T without adding mass.",
        "mechanism": "GeoDAWN Area 2 was flown at 400 m line spacing 150-200 m above terrain, so a lineament's dominant positional error is across strike and of order the 300 m (3 px) kernel radius. The consensus of two independent supplied gradient bands (tmi_hg, iso_grav_anom_hg) is used only as a within-2 px tie-breaker along each dot's local normal (PCA of its 8 nearest parent dots). 22,876 of 37,654 dots moved; mean displacement 2.00 px. The shared collar step removed 1,611 parent dots inside 3 px of the catalogue and refilled the same count from the H19-5 pool, so every arm is identical apart from the dose.",
        "non_fault_confounder": "Lithologic contacts, drainage margins and culture edges also produce magnetic and gravity gradients; a dot can be pulled onto one of those and away from a real fault. The consensus of two independent physical fields reduces, but does not remove, single-source artefacts.",
        "predicted_live_change": "+0.001 DTI (MODEL; plausible -0.010..+0.014)",
        "falsifier": "Paired same-week live receipt against R0 (0.25x step-epsilon dose): Delta <= -0.010 falsifies the cross-strike alignment mechanism at this dose; any |Delta| < 0.010 is no evidence under the session rule. A dose ordering that is not monotone in the cap also falsifies the 'closer to the edge is better' reading, because the two larger caps nest the smaller one.",
        "structural_geologist_note": "Judge whether the moved dots sit on a plausible fault trace or on a stratigraphic contact: the edit never adds mass, so any gain or loss is purely positional. The rank-normalised consensus is a relative edge strength, not a fault probability."
      }
    },
